# Scripts must be standalone

Each task folder here (`ontap-access-review-user/`, `ontap-vol0-check/`, `ontap-cred-rotation/`, …)
holds a script the user runs on a jumphost or VM. Those machines are wiped roughly weekly, and the
way the script gets back onto them is: **open the file, copy the whole thing, paste it into a new
file on the VM, run it.** Nothing else comes across.

So a script must run correctly when it is the only file in an empty directory.

## Not allowed

- Reaching into a sibling folder — no `$SCRIPT_DIR/../other-folder/...`, ever.
- `source`-ing another script for shared functions. Duplicate the function instead; two copies that
  drift are cheaper here than a script that won't start.
- Requiring a sidecar data file to exist. Inventories, cluster lists, secret-name maps, region maps
  and similar reference data belong **inside** the script, as a quoted heredoc:

```bash
read -r -d '' SECRETS_BUILTIN <<'EOF'
cluster-name    SECRET-NAME
EOF
```

## Allowed

- An **optional** external file that adds to the built-in data. Read it on top of the heredoc, never
  instead of it, so creating a one-line file doesn't silently discard what's baked in:

```bash
secrets_map_data() {
  { [[ -f "$SECRETS_MAP" ]] && cat "$SECRETS_MAP"
    printf '%s\n' "$SECRETS_BUILTIN"
  } | awk '$1 !~ /^#/ && NF >= 2 && !seen[tolower($1) " " tolower($2)]++'
}
```

- The user's own `login.sh` for hopping to a jumphost, and ordinary tooling (`ssh`, `sshpass`,
  `gcloud`, `kubectl`, `psql`, `awk`, `python3`). These are environment, not repo dependencies.
- Files the script **writes** (`report/`, `logs/`, `output/`) and genuine per-run input the user
  supplies (a list of accounts to delete). Neither is needed for the script to start.

## When adding a new script

Put it in its own folder with a `README.md`. Say in the `--help` text that it is self-contained and
list the external commands it expects.

`jira-bulk/` is the deliberate exception: it is a reusable tool driven by job directories, not a
copy-to-a-VM script.

## Verify before claiming it works

Copy the script alone into an empty temp directory and run it there:

```bash
rm -rf /tmp/standalone && mkdir -p /tmp/standalone && cp <script> /tmp/standalone/ \
  && (cd /tmp/standalone && bash <script> --help)
```

`bash -n` passing is not enough — it won't catch a missing sidecar file.
